window.docContents[180].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#chapter-02___section-2-14":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory","header":"2.14. Tensor products and duals of representations of Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#chapter-02___section-2-14"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The Lie action on a pure tensor is the sum of the actions on its two factors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.14.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.14. Tensor products and duals of representations of Lie algebras\u0009Tensor product of Lie algebra representations\u0009Tensor product of Lie algebra representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion--Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The algebraic closure of integers modulo a prime has the corresponding characteristic.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.2_intro; role=supporting]\n\nThe algebraic closure of integers modulo a prime is algebraically closed.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.2_intro; role=supporting]\n\nThe finite type cardinality of integers modulo a prime equals that prime.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.2_intro; role=supporting]\n\nA primality witness equips integers modulo that number with a field structure.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.2_intro; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Section 2.2 heading and introduction to systematic discussion\u0009Section 2.2 heading and introduction to systematic discussion\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion--Section-2___2-heading-and-introduction-to-systematic-discussion--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations--Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: LinearEquiv.symm\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.6/Derived4; role=supporting\n\nAn auxiliary predicate on two modules over a common ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.6; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary type associated with two modules over a common ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.6; role=supporting]\n\nA second auxiliary type associated with two modules over a common ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Homomorphism (intertwining operator) of representations\u0009Homomorphism (intertwining operator) of representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations--Homomorphism-_LPAR_intertwining-operator_RPAR_-of-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-implies-indecomposable_SEMI_-main-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable":{"contents":"It is obvious that an irreducible representation is indecomposable. On the other hand, we will see below that the converse statement is false in general.\n\nOne of the main problems of representation theory is to classify irreducible and indecomposable representations of a given algebra up to isomorphism. This problem is usually hard and often can be solved only partially (say, for finite dimensional representations). Below we will see a number of examples in which this problem is partially or fully solved for specific algebras.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Irreducible implies indecomposable; main problems of representation theory","header":"Irreducible implies indecomposable; main problems of representation theory","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-implies-indecomposable_SEMI_-main-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Exercise3___10___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Tensor product of matrix algebras\u0009Tensor product of matrix algebras","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Exercise3___10___1___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-the-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___8___1":{"contents":"Let B = \\bigoplus_{j>1} V_j, B' = \\bigoplus_{j>1} V'_j; then we have V = V_1 \\oplus B = V'_1 \\oplus B'. Consider the map h : B \\to B' defined as a composition of the natural maps B \\to V \\to B' attached to these decompositions. We claim that h is an isomorphism. To show this, it suffices to show that \\ker h = 0 (as h is a map between spaces of the same dimension). Assume that v \\in \\ker h \\subset B. Then v \\in V'_1. On the other hand, the projection of v to V_1 is zero, so gv = 0. Since g is an isomorphism, we get v = 0, as desired.\n\nNow by the induction assumption, m = n, and V_j \\cong V'_{\\sigma(j)} for some permutation \\sigma of 2, \\ldots, n. The theorem is proved. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Completion of proof of the Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"Completion of proof of the Krull-Schmidt theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-the-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___8___1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___9___1":{"contents":"Example 4.9.1. The following tables represent computed tensor product multiplicities of irreducible representations of S_3, S_4, and A_5, respectively:\n\n* S_3* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_-* \\mathbb{C}^2\n* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_-* \\mathbb{C}^2\n* \\mathbb{C}_-* * \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}^2\n* \\mathbb{C}^2* * * \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^2\n\n* S_4* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_-* \\mathbb{C}^2* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* \\mathbb{C}^3_-\n* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}_-* \\mathbb{C}^2* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* \\mathbb{C}^3_-\n* \\mathbb{C}_-* * \\mathbb{C}_+* \\mathbb{C}^2* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}^3_+\n* \\mathbb{C}^2* * * \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^2* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* * * * \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* * * * * \\mathbb{C}_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^2 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-\n\n* A_5* \\mathbb{C}* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}^4* \\mathbb{C}^5\n* \\mathbb{C}* \\mathbb{C}* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}^4* \\mathbb{C}^5\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* * \\mathbb{C} \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+* \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5* \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* * * \\mathbb{C} \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_-* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5\n* \\mathbb{C}^4* * * * \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus \\mathbb{C} \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^5* \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus 2\\mathbb{C}^5 \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^4\n* \\mathbb{C}^5* * * * * \\mathbb{C} \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_+ \\oplus \\mathbb{C}^3_- \\oplus 2\\mathbb{C}^4 \\oplus 2\\mathbb{C}^5\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables\u0009Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5","header":"Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___9___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/End-of-proof-of-Lemma-5___13___1-_LPAR_statement-on-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_end_of_Lemma5___13___1_proof___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009End of proof of Lemma 5.13.1 (statement on missing page)\u0009End of proof of Lemma 5.13.1 (statement on missing page)","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/End-of-proof-of-Lemma-5___13___1-_LPAR_statement-on-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_end_of_Lemma5___13___1_proof___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula--Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials--Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Equates the displayed partition-and-permutation expression with an application of the\ncomplex-valued function.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.15.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Equivalent formulation of Theorem 5.15.1 using Laurent polynomials\u0009Equivalent formulation of Theorem 5.15.1 using Laurent polynomials\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula--Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials--Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___16___-Problems--Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations--Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For each partition of n + 1, the two auxiliary finite-dimensional complex representations of Perm (Fin n) have a nonempty type of isomorphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.16.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.16. Problems\u0009Branching rules: restriction and induction for S_n representations\u0009Branching rules: restriction and induction for S_n representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___16___-Problems--Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations--Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___2":{"contents":"Lemma 6.4.2. (1) B is positive definite.\n\n(2) B(x, x) takes only even values for x \\in \\mathbb{Z}^n.\n\nProof. (1) This follows by definition, since \\Gamma is a Dynkin diagram.\n\n(2) By the definition of the Cartan matrix we get\n\nB(x, x) = x^T A x = \\sum_{i,j} x_i a_{ij} x_j = 2 \\sum_i x_i^2 + \\sum_{i,j,\\ i \\neq j} x_i a_{ij} x_j\n\n= 2 \\sum_i x_i^2 + 2 \\cdot \\sum_{i < j} a_{ij} x_i x_j,\n\nwhich is even. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Lemma 6.4.2: B is positive definite and even-valued","header":"Lemma 6.4.2: B is positive definite and even-valued","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Examples-of-natural-transformations/#Chapter7___Example7___3___2":{"contents":"Example 7.3.2. 1. Let \\mathbf{FVect}_k be the category of finite dimensional vector spaces over k. Then the functors id and ** on this category are isomorphic. The isomorphism is defined by the standard maps a_V : V \\to V^{**} given by a_V(u)(f) = f(u), u \\in V, f \\in V^*. But these two functors are not isomorphic on the category of all vector spaces \\mathbf{Vect}_k, since for an infinite dimensional vector space V, V is not isomorphic to V^{**}.\n\n2. Let \\mathbf{FVect}_k' be the category of finite dimensional k-vector spaces, where the morphisms are the isomorphisms. We have a functor F from this category to itself sending any space V to V^* and any morphism a to (a^*)^{-1}. This functor satisfies the property that V is isomorphic to F(V) for any V, but it is not isomorphic to the identity functor. This is because the isomorphism V \\mapsto F(V) = V^* cannot be chosen to be compatible with the action of GL(V), as V is not isomorphic to V^* as a representation of GL(V).3. Let A be an algebra over a field k, and let F : A - \\mathbf{mod} \\to \\mathbf{Vect}_k be the forgetful functor. Then as follows from Problem 2.3.17, \\operatorname{End} F = \\operatorname{Hom}(F, F) = A.4. The set of endomorphisms of the identity functor on the category A - \\mathbf{mod} is the center of A (check it!).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.3. Morphisms of functors\u0009Examples of natural transformations","header":"Examples of natural transformations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Examples-of-natural-transformations/#Chapter7___Example7___3___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Finitely-generated-modules-over-finitely-generated-commutative-ring/#Chapter7___Problem7___7___3":{"contents":"Problem 7.7.3. Let A be a finitely generated commutative ring. Show that the category of finitely generated A-modules is an abelian category.\n\nHint: Use the Hilbert basis theorem.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.7. Abelian categories\u0009Finitely generated modules over finitely generated commutative ring","header":"Finitely generated modules over finitely generated commutative ring","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Finitely-generated-modules-over-finitely-generated-commutative-ring/#Chapter7___Problem7___7___3"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem--Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A projective resolution of the coefficient module object selected by a finite basis.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nThe terms of the basis-indexed projective resolution are isomorphic to the corresponding graded module objects.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nThe augmentation of the basis-indexed projective resolution is a quasi-isomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nA basis-indexed isomorphism from the displayed module construction to the dual of an exterior power.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nA basis-indexed isomorphism from the displayed module construction to an exterior power.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nA basis-indexed projective resolution of the displayed module over a symmetric algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nEvery degree of the complex underlying the basis-indexed auxiliary projective resolution is free over the symmetric algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary projective resolution of the displayed module associated with a finite-dimensional module and a second module over a field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nEach component of the finite-dimensional auxiliary projective resolution is isomorphic to the displayed module over the symmetric algebra on the product module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nThe augmentation of the finite-dimensional auxiliary projective resolution is a quasi-isomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nConstructs an auxiliary projective resolution for the displayed module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nShows that the displayed resolution has zero components above the finite rank.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nAbove the cardinality of a chosen basis, the specified indexed object associated with a right module and a left module is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\nExtension classes between modules over a symmetric algebra are subsingletons in degrees above the cardinality of a chosen basis.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.10; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Koszul resolution and Hilbert syzygies theorem\u0009Koszul resolution and Hilbert syzygies theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem--Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Hom-from-projective-cover-computes-Jordan-Holder-multiplicity/#Chapter9___Proposition9___2___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Hom from projective cover computes Jordan-Holder multiplicity\u0009Hom from projective cover computes Jordan-Holder multiplicity","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Hom-from-projective-cover-computes-Jordan-Holder-multiplicity/#Chapter9___Proposition9___2___3___formalization"}});